<HelpLayout title="Gyms & Equipment" description="How to manage gyms, machines, attachments, and grip types in Badger.">
  <h1>Gyms & Equipment</h1>

  <p>Badger lets you track which gym, machine, and attachment you used for each exercise. This unlocks machine-scoped progress tracking - your cable row on the low pulley and your cable row on the high cable are treated as separate performance histories.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Managing gyms</h2>
  <p>Go to <strong>You → Gyms</strong> to manage your gym list.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a gym.</li>
    <li>Tap a gym to edit its name or notes, or to set it as the default.</li>
    <li>The <strong>default gym</strong> is automatically used when starting a workout if you have only one gym, or if a default is set among multiple.</li>
    <li>Each gym has its own <strong>Plate inventory</strong> - open a gym and tap <em>Plate assortment</em> to set which plates (and how many) are available there. Competition gyms often stock both kg and lbs plates, so the set is kept per unit. New gyms start from your global default set, so the plate calculator works out of the box; per-gym editing is optional.</li>
    <li>Gyms can be <strong>archived</strong> (hidden from pickers but preserved for historical data) instead of deleted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="machines">Machines</h2>
  <p>Go to <strong>You → Machines</strong> to manage machines.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a machine (e.g. "Cable station", "Leg press", "Smith machine"). Give it a name and pick which gyms it lives at - if you only have one gym, it is linked there automatically and Badger doesn't ask. You land on the new machine's page straight away, so you can set the rest of its details while you're still standing in front of it.</li>
    <li>Each machine has two toggles: <strong>Uses attachments</strong> and <strong>Uses grip types</strong>.</li>
    <li>Set the machine's <strong>Resistance type</strong> - <em>Selectorized</em> (a pin-selected weight stack), <em>Plate-loaded</em>, or <em>Unspecified</em> if you'd rather not say. This is the setting that matters most: it decides what the rest of the machine's weight settings even ask you.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Plate calculator</strong> section follows the resistance type, so you're only ever asked something that makes sense for the machine in front of you:
      <ul>
        <li><em>Selectorized</em> - there is no plate calculator section at all. A pin-selected stack has no plates to load, so there is nothing to configure. The weight increment settings below stay, since those are exactly what a stack does need.</li>
        <li><em>Plate-loaded</em> - you choose <em>Dual-Loaded</em> or <em>Single-Loaded</em>. It starts on Dual-Loaded, which is what a machine that has never been configured already behaved as. Dual-Loaded also lets you enter the machine's <strong>Starting weight</strong> (see below). Turning the calculator off isn't offered here: a machine you load with plates has plate math by definition.</li>
        <li><em>Unspecified</em> - you choose <em>None</em>, <em>Dual-Loaded</em> or <em>Single-Loaded</em>, and nothing is selected until you pick one. Until then Badger says so ("Not set") and the exercise's own plate calculator setting applies. <em>None</em> turns the calculator off for this machine entirely.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>The <strong>Starting weight</strong> is what the machine weighs empty - the bar, the sled, or the arms, before any plates. It used to be called "Bar weight", which only named half of what it is: plenty of machines have no bar at all. Most aren't labelled either, so it is completely fine to leave it blank - the plate calculator then just counts plates. Tap the <strong>(i)</strong> beside the label for the same explanation in the app.</li>
    <li>On a plate-loaded machine, <strong>How weight is logged</strong> says what a number you type on this machine <em>means</em>: the <strong>Total</strong>, or what is on <strong>one side</strong>. Iso-lateral machines are the reason it exists - if you load 90&nbsp;lbs a side and write down 90, Badger should know that is one side and not quietly file it as 90 total. It applies wherever the machine is used: the weight field says "/ side", the plate calculator reads your number the same way, and every set you log on it is recorded with the answer. Nothing is doubled or converted - your numbers are still exactly the numbers you wrote - and sets you logged before this existed are left alone rather than guessed at. It is only asked for plate-loaded machines: a pin-selected stack has one number and no sides.</li>
    <li>Set a <strong>Logging unit</strong> if this machine should be logged in a specific unit (kg or lbs) regardless of your app setting - useful for a stack that is physically kg but dual-marked with awkward lbs numbers. Leave it on <em>Default</em> to follow the exercise, then the app setting. When you start a set on the machine, the weight field switches to its unit automatically - and so do the routine editor, quick edit, and the log preview for any routine slot that has this machine as its default, so you plan in the unit you lift in.</li>
    <li>Optionally set a <strong>Weight increment</strong> - the physical step between settings (for example 5&nbsp;kg between stack positions), entered in the machine's own unit. For stacks that change step size partway up, also fill in <strong>Then step</strong> and <strong>Above</strong> (for example 10&nbsp;kg up to 100, then 20&nbsp;kg). The progressive-overload suggestion snaps to this grid, and so do the <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> buttons beside the weight field while you log - so a stack that steps by 10 moves by 10, not by your exercise's 2.5.</li>
    <li>On the machine's detail screen, use the <strong>Available at</strong> section to link the machine to one or more gyms. A machine that is linked to no gym at all can't be offered while you're training, and the section tells you so.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleting a machine:</strong> if the machine isn't referenced anywhere, deleting it also removes it from every gym and exercise link. If it's used in a logged workout or set as a default in a routine, deletion is blocked so your history and routines stay intact - remove it from those first if you really want it gone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Linking machines to exercises</h3>
  <p>Linking a machine to an exercise marks the two as compatible, which is what puts the machine at the top of the "Set machine" picker while you train.</p>
  <p>You don't have to set this up in advance. The picker has a second section, <strong>Other machines at this gym</strong>, listing everything else at your current gym. Pick one and Badger links it to the exercise for you, so it's in the top section from then on. Do the linking whenever it suits you: while training, or up front via the exercise library → <strong>Edit</strong> → <strong>Compatible machines</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Attachments</h2>
  <p>Attachments are cable/pulley accessories (e.g. "Straight bar", "Rope", "V-bar", "D-handle"). Go to <strong>You → Attachments</strong> to manage them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create an attachment. Like machines, you name it and pick its gyms (skipped when you only have one), then land on its page to finish up.</li>
    <li>Link attachments to gyms via the <strong>Available at</strong> section on each attachment's detail screen. An attachment with no gym isn't offered while training, and the section says so.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleting an attachment</strong> works like deleting a machine: blocked if it appears in a logged workout or a routine default, otherwise removed from all gyms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Attachments are available in the machine picker only when the current machine has <strong>Uses attachments</strong> enabled.</p>
  <p>Notes you write on an attachment show up in the training screen's notes card whenever that attachment is selected, next to the machine's own setup notes.</p>

  <h2 id="photos">Photos</h2>
  <p>A machine or attachment can carry a photo, so you can recognize the right piece of kit when you're standing in front of the rack. Open the machine or attachment and tap the photo panel at the top to <strong>take a photo</strong> or <strong>choose one from your gallery</strong>. Tap it again to replace or remove it.</p>
  <p>Attachment photos also show as a small thumbnail in the attachment picker during a workout, which is the point: "the rope or the short V-bar?" is easier to answer from a picture than a name.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photos stay on your device unless you ask otherwise.</strong> Nothing is uploaded on its own, and Badger only asks for camera or photo access at the moment you first use it.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud backup leaves photos out unless you turn them on.</strong> Backups stay small and quick by default, so photos are not included and a restore onto a new phone brings your machines and attachments back without their pictures. To carry them across, turn on <strong>Back up equipment photos</strong> on the <a href={`/help/settings#cloud-backup`}>Cloud backup</a> screen. It is off by default, because photos make backups considerably larger and they use your own Drive storage.</li>
    <li>Deleting a machine or attachment deletes its photo too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="grip-types">Grip types</h2>
  <p>When a machine has <strong>Uses grip types</strong> enabled, a row of grip chips appears in the training screen for that exercise. Select your grip before or after logging sets:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Overhand</li>
    <li>Underhand</li>
    <li>Neutral</li>
    <li>Wide</li>
    <li>Close</li>
    <li>Hammer</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tap the active chip again to clear the selection. The grip is stored on the workout exercise record and shown in the history tab.</p>
  <p>You can also set a <strong>default grip type</strong> per routine exercise in the predefined sets screen - this gets pre-filled when starting a routine workout.</p>

  <h2 id="workout-context">Gym context during workouts</h2>

  <h3>Automatic gym assignment</h3>
  <p>When a workout is created, Badger tries to set the gym automatically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If you have only one gym → that gym is used.</li>
    <li>If you have a default gym → that gym is used.</li>
    <li>If you have multiple gyms with no default → no gym is set (you can assign it manually).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a routine day has a <strong>default gym</strong> configured, that overrides the above when starting from a routine.</p>

  <h3>Changing the gym mid-workout</h3>
  <p>The current gym name appears in the training screen's header bar. To change it: tap <strong>⋮ → Change gym…</strong> and select a different gym.</p>

  <h3>Setting a machine for an exercise</h3>
  <p>Tap <strong>⋮ → Set machine…</strong> to pick a machine and (if applicable) an attachment. The top of the picker lists machines already marked compatible with this exercise at your current gym. Below that, <strong>Other machines at this gym</strong> lists the rest, so nothing at the gym is out of reach - and picking from it marks that machine compatible for next time.</p>
  <p>Badger remembers the last attachment used for each exercise + machine combination and pre-selects it next time.</p>

  <h2>Progress filtering by machine</h2>
  <p>On the Progress screen, if you've used the same exercise on multiple machines, a row of filter chips appears. Select a specific machine to see only that machine's history, or select <strong>All</strong> to see combined data.</p>
  <p>The most recently used machine is selected by default. The same filter applies to PRs - they're scoped per machine when a machine is set.</p>

  <h2>Routine defaults</h2>
  <p>You can set default equipment at the routine level to avoid re-selecting it each session:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Routine day → Default gym</strong> - Pre-sets the workout gym when starting from this day.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine exercise → Default machine & attachment</strong> - Pre-sets the machine for that exercise when starting from a routine.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine exercise → Default grip type</strong> - Pre-sets the grip for that exercise.</li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>
